**Model.** `Show` has a list of `Seat`s. Each seat has a state: `AVAILABLE`, `HELD` (with owner and expiry) or `BOOKED`. A user books *k* specific seats or any *k* seats.

**Core invariant:** a seat is never given to two users. Protect it at the finest sensible granularity.

**Approach 1: per-seat atomic state.** Keep an `AtomicReference<SeatState>` (or a `ReentrantLock`) per seat. To book several seats:
1. Sort the seat ids and lock/CAS in that order. A global ordering prevents deadlock between two users who want overlapping seats.
2. If any seat is not available, roll back the ones you took and fail. All-or-nothing.
3. On success, mark them `HELD`, take payment, then mark `BOOKED`. A background task releases expired holds.

**Approach 2: counting for "any k seats".** A `Semaphore(capacity)` per show with `tryAcquire(k)` rejects early when the show is full. It only counts capacity; you still need to choose concrete seats safely.

```java
boolean book(Show s, List<Integer> ids, String user) {
  List<Integer> sorted = ids.stream().sorted().toList();
  List<Seat> got = new ArrayList<>();
  for (int id : sorted) {
    Seat seat = s.seat(id);
    if (seat.tryHold(user)) got.add(seat);   // CAS AVAILABLE -> HELD
    else { got.forEach(x -> x.release(user)); return false; }
  }
  got.forEach(x -> x.confirm(user));
  return true;
}
```

**Where `CyclicBarrier` fits:** mostly in your test harness. N threads wait at the barrier and then all call `book` for the same seat at the same instant. Assert that exactly one succeeds. It is not normally needed in the production path, and saying so shows you understand the tool.

**Pitfalls to name:** check-then-act races (`if available then book` without atomicity), holding a lock during slow payment calls, forgetting to release on exceptions (use `finally`), and unbounded holds.

**Beyond one JVM:** use a DB row-level conditional update (`UPDATE seat SET status='HELD' WHERE id=? AND status='AVAILABLE'`) or a distributed lock with TTL.
